ts from the TV networks and I  think the NFLPA leadership at the very  top the executive director d'amour Smith  has decided everyone gets more if they  do a deal now and they do TV deals  premise on 10 years of labor piece  versus slamming the brakes on everything  and getting maybe less or TV deals now  or lesser TV deals later because of the  specter of a potential work stoppage and  you know your autumn Utley gonna do a  deal based on 17 games when do you do it  and I think demora Smith has made the  judgment right or wrong that everyone  gets a better deal if they get this done  now so then they can maximize the TV  money yeah I just don't understand the  10-year deal I mean it feels like that  this it's gonna be antiquated here like  after five years  well I agree but you know what the more  its premise on a true sharing of the  revenue and they're getting closer and  closer to 5050 48.5 is what the split  will ultimately be now you have to get  your arms around the exemptions and the  exceptions and the stuff they take off  the top and that was a sticking point in  the summertime it's it hasn't been  mentioned much recently but I think you  know the question to be extent to which  the players will help fund new stadiums  that apparently is been resolved you  don't hear anybody complaining about  where things stand on that but as it as  it becomes a true partnership and and  you're sharing the money now the owners  always have the equity and the players  have none which gets overlooked I mean  in the value of the franchise that keeps  skyrocketing but the closer you come to  a 50/50 split of the revenue the more  you can feel like it's a true  partnership and and and I know that  there are some within ownership that  want to approach it that way but there's  also a group of hardliners who their  attitude is screw them lock them out  there they work for us we set the terms  we want 18 games we want this we want  that and and there's kind of a there's  not as yo